    "['set protocols bgp group [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]'}, {'para_map': [0, 0], 'trans_command': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor [parameter1] activate', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'address-family ipv4 unicast'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'no synchronization', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'address-family [parameter1]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]'}]]": 1,
    "['type [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp 100', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 10.2.1.2 remote-as 100\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco, you don\\'t explicitly declare a group as \"external\" like in Juniper. Instead, you configure BGP neighbors with their AS numbers, and if the neighbor\\'s AS is different from the local router\\'s AS, it\\'s automatically treated as an external peer. In this case, since both peers are in AS 100 (based on the Juniper configuration), it would technically be an iBGP session in Cisco. There might be a discrepancy here as the Juniper config shows \"type external\" but peer-as 100 (same as local AS).', 'trans_template': 'router bgp 100'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 10.2.1.2 remote-as 100', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Juniper configuration:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 10.2.1.2 remote-as 100\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco, you don\\'t explicitly declare a group as \"external\" like in Juniper. Instead, you configure BGP neighbors with their AS numbers, and if the neighbor\\'s AS is different from the local router\\'s AS, it\\'s automatically treated as an external peer. In this case, since both peers are in AS 100 (based on the Juniper configuration), it would technically be an iBGP session in Cisco. There might be a discrepancy here as the Juniper config shows \"type external\" but peer-as 100 (same as local AS).', 'trans_template': 'neighbor 10.2.1.2 remote-as 100'}]]": 1,
    "['peer-as [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp 100', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nrouter bgp 100\\nneighbor 10.2.1.2 remote-as 100\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco, the `peer-as` is specified both in the `router bgp` statement (local AS) and in the `neighbor remote-as` statement (peer AS). In this case, since the peer AS is the same as the local AS in the original Juniper configuration, both values are set to 100. The neighbor IP is taken from the subsequent `set protocols bgp group external-peers neighbor 10.2.1.2` command in the original configuration.', 'trans_template': 'router bgp 100'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 10.2.1.2 remote-as 100', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nrouter bgp 100\\nneighbor 10.2.1.2 remote-as 100\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco, the `peer-as` is specified both in the `router bgp` statement (local AS) and in the `neighbor remote-as` statement (peer AS). In this case, since the peer AS is the same as the local AS in the original Juniper configuration, both values are set to 100. The neighbor IP is taken from the subsequent `set protocols bgp group external-peers neighbor 10.2.1.2` command in the original configuration.', 'trans_template': 'neighbor 10.2.1.2 remote-as 100'}]]": 1,
    "['neighbor [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'router bgp 100', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco configuration for the specified Juniper commands:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 10.2.1.2 remote-as 100\\n neighbor 10.2.1.2 address-family ipv4 unicast\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco IOS:\\n1. The first command establishes the BGP neighbor relationship (equivalent to Juniper's peer-as setting)\\n2. The second command enables IPv4 unicast address family for the neighbor (equivalent to Juniper's family inet unicast setting)\\n3. Unlike Juniper where peer-as is set at the group level, Cisco requires it to be specified per neighbor\", 'trans_template': 'router bgp 100'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 10.2.1.2 remote-as 100', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco configuration for the specified Juniper commands:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 10.2.1.2 remote-as 100\\n neighbor 10.2.1.2 address-family ipv4 unicast\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco IOS:\\n1. The first command establishes the BGP neighbor relationship (equivalent to Juniper's peer-as setting)\\n2. The second command enables IPv4 unicast address family for the neighbor (equivalent to Juniper's family inet unicast setting)\\n3. Unlike Juniper where peer-as is set at the group level, Cisco requires it to be specified per neighbor\", 'trans_template': 'neighbor 10.2.1.2 remote-as 100'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'neighbor 10.2.1.2 address-family ipv4 unicast', 'parent_command': ['router bgp [parameter1]', 'neighbor [parameter1] remote-as [parameter2]'], 'root': 'router bgp [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the equivalent Cisco configuration for the specified Juniper commands:\\n\\n##\\nrouter bgp 100\\n neighbor 10.2.1.2 remote-as 100\\n neighbor 10.2.1.2 address-family ipv4 unicast\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco IOS:\\n1. The first command establishes the BGP neighbor relationship (equivalent to Juniper's peer-as setting)\\n2. The second command enables IPv4 unicast address family for the neighbor (equivalent to Juniper's family inet unicast setting)\\n3. Unlike Juniper where peer-as is set at the group level, Cisco requires it to be specified per neighbor\", 'trans_template': 'neighbor 10.2.1.2 address-family ipv4 unicast'}]]": 1,
